    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ssc_111'), 'The content-tools build depends on @fastgrade/runner. After installing 4.2.1:

$ npm audit signatures
audited 419 packages in 2s
419 packages have verified registry signatures
37 packages have verified attestations

The attestation of @fastgrade/runner@4.2.1 says:
  source:   github.com/fastgrade/runner @ commit 9f3c2e1
  workflow: .github/workflows/release.yml (GitHub Actions)

$ git log -1 --format=''%h %an %s'' 9f3c2e1   (in the fastgrade/runner repository)
9f3c2e1 maint-bot chore: bump deps

The commit was pushed directly to main; no pull request references it.

Statement: "The verified attestation shows that 4.2.1 contains only code that was reviewed in the fastgrade/runner repository."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Provenance links the tarball to a repository, a commit and a workflow, and the signature proves that link was not forged after the build. It says nothing about whether the commit was reviewed or written by the maintainers: here commit 9f3c2e1 was pushed straight to main, which is exactly what an attacker with a stolen maintainer token can do, and the release workflow would sign the result just the same. What the attestation does give you is where to look: the commit and the workflow run. The misconception is that "verified" means "safe"; it means "built where it says it was built".', 1, 5
