Actions log, Thursday run 18902:
  Download action repository ''cachekit/setup-cache@v3'' (SHA:e93d51f08a2c7b4e6f1d0a9c3b85e2f7d4a6c019)

The cachekit/setup-cache repository shows no new release between Monday and Thursday.

Statement: "The code that ran as cachekit/setup-cache@v3 changed between Monday and Thursday although the workflow file did not, and pinning the step to the full commit SHA from Monday would have kept Monday''s code."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. A tag such as v3 is a movable pointer in someone else''s repository: the log shows it resolved to two different commits, and with no release in between, someone moved it. A full-length commit SHA is immutable, so `uses: cachekit/setup-cache@4b1e0c7a92f5d3e8a1c6b0f2d9e47a3c5b8f1d20` runs the same code until you change the line in a reviewed pull request. With `permissions: write-all`, the changed action could also push to the repository or create a release with the job''s token, which is why lesson 9 narrows permissions per job. The misconception is that a version-looking tag is as fixed as a version number on the registry.', 2, 5
