WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ssc_311'), 'Pull request #482 "Add S3 upload for course covers", branch feat/cover-upload, pushed to GitHub:

a1c9e02  add S3 client     + const accessKeyId = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE";
b7d3f11  fix lint
c04e8aa  move key to env   - const accessKeyId = "AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE";
                           + const accessKeyId = process.env.AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID!;

CI job "secrets" (scans git diff origin/main...HEAD at c04e8aa): 0 findings
Merge method selected: Squash and merge

Statement: "Because the pull request will be squash-merged and the scanned diff has no key, the key never left the branch in a usable form, so it does not need to be revoked."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'False. Commit a1c9e02 was pushed, so the key is on GitHub: in the branch, in refs/pull/482/head (which GitHub keeps even after the branch is deleted), in every clone and fork, and in any CI log or cache built from that commit. Squashing changes what lands on main, not what was already published. The scanner found nothing because it scanned the net diff origin/main...HEAD, where the add and the remove cancel out. The fix is to revoke the key in AWS and issue a new one, then scan every pushed commit (or block the push with push protection), not only the final diff. The misconception is that deleting a secret in a later commit, or squashing, undoes the leak; once a secret is pushed, assume it is known and rotate it.', 1, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ssc_311') AND order_index = 1)
    RETURNING id
)
