WITH q AS (
    INSERT INTO challenge.questions (challenge_id, question, type, explanation, order_index, points)
    SELECT (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ssc_311'), 'Added line in src/lib/storage.ts:
+const secretAccessKey = process.env.S3_SECRET ?? "wJalrXUtnFEMI/K7MDENG/bPxRfiCYEXAMPLEKEY";

Scanner output:
src/lib/storage.ts:5 high-entropy-base64 wJal... (40 chars)

Author''s reply in the review: "False positive - production sets S3_SECRET, the string is only a fallback."

Statement: "The finding is real: the literal after ?? is part of the source and the build output, and it is the key the code uses whenever S3_SECRET is unset."', 'single_choice'::question_type, 'True. The ?? operator does not hide the string: it is committed in the repository, compiled into the bundle and shipped wherever the code runs, and it becomes the active credential in any environment that forgets S3_SECRET (a preview deploy, a new region, a local run). An entropy rule exists for exactly this case: no known prefix matched, but a 40-character base64 string with 4.66 bits per character is very unlikely to be text a person wrote. The fix is to remove the fallback and fail at startup when the variable is missing, then rotate the key. The misconception is that reading the environment first makes a hard-coded default harmless; a secret in a fallback is still a secret in the code.', 2, 5
    WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM challenge.questions WHERE challenge_id = (SELECT id FROM challenge.challenges WHERE public_id = 'chal_quiz_ssc_311') AND order_index = 2)
    RETURNING id
)
